From cb535b372e41a7dc8f6b39606d9da22c2d8a09f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gigi <42325924+g-cqd@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 22:07:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] fix(d3d11): drop draws whose render pipeline failed to
 compile

When a pipeline failed to compile, the set-pipeline lambda returned without
setting anything, but the draw after it was still encoded, so it ran with
whatever pipeline the encoder had bound before and with bindings laid out
for other shaders. Crysis 3 hits this on M1/M2: a pixel shader reads a
varying its geometry shader does not write, Apple's compiler service
crashes on the VS+GS mesh pipeline ("Failed to create mesh PSO: ...
XPC_ERROR_CONNECTION_INTERRUPTED"), the GS draw runs with a stale mesh
pipeline, the GPU page-faults, and every later command buffer is ignored.

The set-pipeline lambdas of the ordinary, geometry and tessellation paths
now record a failed pipeline in RenderEncoderData::pipeline_failed, and all
14 draw paths go through EmitDrawOP, which drops the draw while it is set.
A warning is logged once. The mesh pipeline errors now name the shaders
(VS=xxxxxxxx GS=... PS=..., the SHA-1 prefixes used in the shader cache
function names) so the failing combination can be found.

tests/dx11/dx11_failed_pipeline draws a valid pipeline (red, left half) and
an unlinkable one (right half) in one render pass, for VS->PS and
VS->GS->PS. Before: the right half is red on i686 and x86_64 (the failed
draw ran with the previous pipeline). After: it stays black. Also fixes
the meson.build entry of dx11_staging_rename (unquoted names).
